>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters are often considered for the same rooms.</p>
  <p>Both are quiet electric resistance heaters commonly used in <a href="/categories/bedroom/">bedrooms</a>, <a href="/categories/home-office/">home offices</a> and smaller enclosed living areas. Neither normally relies on a strong fan, so both provide gradual background warmth rather than an immediate blast of hot air.</p>
  <p>The practical differences are the way the heat feels, how quickly it is noticed, how much warmth the heater retains, and whether portability or a shallow wall position matters more.</p>
  <p>Slimness, oil or “eco” wording does not prove lower electricity use. Compare rated wattage, thermostat behaviour, controls and a safe position before choosing either type.</p>

  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater can suit a small enclosed room where quiet, gradual warmth is wanted and there is safe floor space. A panel heater can suit a bedroom or compact office where a slim profile or approved wall mounting is useful.</p>
  <p>Neither is an efficient answer for heating several connected rooms or a large open-plan area. Both convert electricity into resistance heat, so a model that runs at a high wattage for a long time can use substantial electricity regardless of its shape.</p>
  <p>Choose for the room, placement and operating pattern. Quiet operation does not make either heater suitable for unattended overnight use.</p>

  <h2>How oil column heaters work</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater contains an electrical element and sealed heat-transfer oil. The element warms the oil, which transfers heat to the metal columns. The heater then warms the room through natural convection and radiant warmth from its surface.</p>
  <p>The oil is not burned or normally consumed, and it does not require routine topping up. It acts as thermal mass: it takes time to heat, then continues releasing some warmth as the element cycles off and the body cools.</p>
  <p>Most models are portable and have castors, several heat settings and a thermostat. Some add a timer, digital controls, cord storage, overheat protection or a tip-over switch. The body is relatively bulky and can be heavy to move.</p>

  <h2>How panel heaters work</h2>
  <p>A panel heater is a slim electric resistance heater that mainly warms air by natural convection. Cooler air enters around the lower part of the heater, warms inside or behind the panel and rises into the room.</p>
  <p>Many models mount on a wall; others can stand on manufacturer-supplied feet. Controls may be mechanical or digital. Most operate without a fan, although a model with a boost fan will feel and sound different.</p>
  <p>A wall-mounted panel can preserve floor space, but it still needs clear airflow and the manufacturer’s specified clearances. Panel heaters must not be blocked by furniture or curtains.</p>

