  <h2>Heat feel, warm-up time and retained warmth</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater provides both convection and some direct radiant warmth. A person sitting within a suitable distance may notice warmth from the body before the whole room reaches a stable temperature. The internal oil and metal must warm first, so the initial response can feel slow.</p>
  <p>A panel heater relies more on rising warm air. It may begin warming that air sooner, but the room still heats gradually through circulation. A thin panel is not automatically a fast heater.</p>
  <p>Neither type normally matches the immediate effect of a <a href="/guides/fan-heater-vs-ceramic-heater/">fan-assisted heater</a>. They make more sense when the room can warm before comfort is urgently needed.</p>
  <p>An oil column heater stays warm for a period after its element switches off because the oil and metal have stored heat. That retained warmth is not free heat: it came from electricity already used. Thermal mass changes when heat enters the room, not the total heat produced from the electrical input.</p>

  <h2>Electricity use and rated wattage</h2>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters are both electric resistance heaters. If a 2000-watt oil column heater and a 2000-watt panel heater operate continuously at full input for the same time, their electricity consumption will be broadly similar.</p>
  <p>Actual use over a session depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>rated input and selected heat setting</li>
    <li>how long the element operates</li>
    <li>thermostat cycling and target temperature</li>
    <li>room size, starting temperature and heat loss</li>
    <li>how the household uses the controls</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A lower heat setting usually reduces electrical input and heat output while selected. It does not change resistance heating into a more efficient process. A heater that is too small for the room may run continuously without delivering comfortable warmth.</p>
  <p>Do not infer low electricity use from slimness, the presence of oil, wall mounting, the number of columns or words such as “eco”. Check the rated watts for each setting and any boost mode.</p>

  <h2>Thermostats and controls</h2>
  <p>The thermostat switches the element on and off in response to its temperature sensor. A basic mechanical dial may not correspond to a precise room temperature. A digital display may offer a target temperature, but its accuracy still depends on sensor position, nearby warm air, cold walls and room airflow.</p>
  <p>Useful controls can include stepped heat settings, a clear thermostat, a timer, scheduling and a simple manual override. In a bedroom or office, also consider display brightness, control beeps and whether settings remain after a power interruption.</p>
  <p>Check whether the heater automatically restarts when power returns. Timers, thermostats, child locks, overheat protection and tip-over switches are backup or control features; they do not justify unattended operation, blocked airflow or reduced clearances.</p>

  <h2>Noise</h2>
  <p>Both types are generally quieter than fan heaters. An oil column model may click as the thermostat cycles or tick as metal expands and cools. A panel heater may produce relay clicks, control beeps, expansion sounds or vibration against its brackets. A boost fan adds fan noise.</p>
  <p>Sounds that seem minor in a shop can be noticeable in a quiet bedroom or office. Check whether beeps and display lighting can be disabled. Quiet operation does not make either heater suitable for use while sleeping.</p>

  <h2>Floor space, wall mounting and placement</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater needs a flat, stable floor position outside walkways and clear of doors, desks, paper, bedding, curtains and furniture. Its castors make it movable, but do not pull it by the cord or move it while the columns are hot.</p>
  <p>A panel heater can keep the floor clear when correctly wall-mounted. The wall must allow the required space above, below and beside the unit, free convection airflow, access to controls and a suitable power supply. Do not place it behind a sofa, desk, open door or floor-length curtains.</p>
  <p>Wall mounting must follow the heater instructions and property approval requirements. Renters should obtain written approval before drilling or permanently mounting a heater. Use the supplied brackets or feet; do not improvise supports. Any required fixed electrical work belongs with an appropriately licensed electrician.</p>

  <h2>Portability and storage</h2>
  <p>Oil column heaters are usually designed to move between suitable rooms, but their weight, width and hot surfaces can make them awkward. Check the assembled weight, handles, castors, cord storage and whether stairs are involved.</p>
  <p>A portable panel model may be lighter, but it must use the compatible feet and remain stable on the intended floor. A wall-mounted model is not portable simply because it can be removed from brackets.</p>
  <p>Before storage, switch the heater off, unplug it, let it cool and inspect the cord and plug. Clean and store it in the orientation specified by the manufacturer, without heavy items resting on it.</p>

  <h2>Bedrooms, offices and small enclosed rooms</h2>
  <p>Both heater types are best considered for a small or moderate enclosed room where the door can be closed and warmth can build gradually. Large windows, high ceilings, frequent door opening and significant draughts increase the heating task.</p>
  <p>In a bedroom, an oil column heater may suit supervised pre-bedtime warming where safe floor space is available. A panel heater may suit where an approved wall position preserves circulation space. Neither should sit within the clearance zone of bedding or curtains.</p>
  <p>In a home office, either may provide quiet background warmth. Check that an oil heater will not crowd the chair or cables, and that a panel will not be obstructed by the desk, papers or storage.</p>
  <p>For a large open room, several connected spaces or regular long-duration heating, compare other <a href="/guides/home-heating-options-australia/">home heating options</a>. A suitable reverse-cycle system can provide substantially more efficient regular room heating than electric resistance heaters.</p>

  <h2>Portable-heater safety</h2>
  <p>Portable heaters should plug directly into a suitable wall power point. Use one appliance per power point. Do not use a power board, double adaptor or joined extension leads, and do not run the cord beneath a rug, through a doorway or where it can be damaged.</p>
  <p>Keep the heater at least one metre from bedding, curtains, furniture and clothing. Keep paper, towels, drying racks and other combustible items outside that clearance as well, and follow any greater clearance in the manufacturer’s instructions.</p>
  <p>Never cover either heater or use it to dry clothing. Panel heaters must not be blocked by furniture or curtains because their convection inlets and outlets need clear airflow.</p>
  <p>Switch heaters off before leaving the room or sleeping. Quiet operation, a timer or a thermostat does not make unattended overnight use appropriate. Maintain working smoke alarms and supervise children or pets around operating heaters and hot surfaces.</p>
  <p>Stop using a heater if its cord, plug or casing is damaged, if the power point becomes unusually hot or discoloured, or if there is sparking, intermittent operation or a burning smell. A damaged or unsuitable outlet should be assessed by a licensed electrician. Check the model against current product recalls.</p>

  <h2>Which type is likely to suit?</h2>
  <p>An oil column heater may suit when quiet gradual warmth, some radiant feel and portability are useful, and a stable floor position can remain clear. It is less compelling when immediate heat, minimal storage space or easy movement on stairs matters.</p>
  <p>A panel heater may suit when a shallow profile, quiet convection and approved wall mounting are priorities. It is less compelling when the available wall is behind furniture or curtains, mounting is not permitted, or the heater needs to move regularly.</p>
  <p>If neither has a safe position or the room remains difficult to warm, address the room or choose a more appropriate system rather than buying a higher-wattage portable heater by default.</p>

  <h2>Before You Buy Checklist</h2>
  <p>Before choosing an oil column or panel heater, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list" aria-label="Oil column and panel heater checklist">
    <li>Is the room small or moderate in size and able to be enclosed?</li>
    <li>Is gradual background warmth suitable, or is rapid heat required?</li>
    <li>What is the rated wattage at each heat setting?</li>
    <li>How clearly does the thermostat control and display temperature?</li>
    <li>Does the heater restart automatically after a power interruption?</li>
    <li>Are timer, display and control sounds suitable for the room?</li>
    <li>Is quiet operation important without implying overnight use?</li>
    <li>Is there a stable, clear floor position for an oil column heater?</li>
    <li>Can the oil heater be moved and stored safely?</li>
    <li>Is there a suitable unobstructed wall for a panel heater?</li>
    <li>Will furniture and curtains remain clear of the panel?</li>
    <li>Do mounting instructions suit the wall and supplied brackets?</li>
    <li>Is written property approval required for wall mounting?</li>
    <li>Can the heater plug directly into a suitable wall power point?</li>
    <li>Will it be the only appliance connected to that power point?</li>
    <li>Can the cord remain clear of rugs, doorways and walkways?</li>
    <li>Can at least one metre be kept clear around the heater?</li>
    <li>Will the heater remain uncovered and away from drying clothes?</li>
    <li>Will it be switched off before leaving the room or sleeping?</li>
    <li>Are the cord, plug, casing and safety features in sound condition?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choose for the room, not the shape</h2>
  <p>Oil column and panel heaters can both provide quiet, gradual warmth in a suitable enclosed room. An oil column heater adds portability, a more radiant feel and retained warmth; a panel heater offers a shallow profile and can preserve floor space when correctly mounted.</p>
  <p>Neither design is inherently cheap to run. Compare rated power, operating time, thermostat behaviour and the available safe position, then choose the heater that fits the room without being blocked or left unattended.</p>

